<DocsLayout
  title="Stereo Workflow — MHA Manager"
  description="How CalibrateStereo, Identity Create, and stereo performance jobs chain together — queue a whole actor in any order and let prerequisites resolve."
  currentSlug="stereo-workflow"
>

  <div class="doc-header">
    <h1 class="doc-title">Stereo Workflow</h1>
    <p class="doc-lead">
      A stereo (HMC) performance take is the highest-fidelity job type, but it cannot solve alone: it
      needs a <strong>camera calibration</strong> and a prepared <strong>MetaHuman Identity</strong>
      for its actor first. The queue produces both as their own job types and chains them for you —
      so you can queue an entire shoot in any order and let the dependencies resolve.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section class="doc-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">The parent chain</h2>
    <p class="section-desc">
      Stereo is a family of three job types in a strict dependency order. The first two exist only to
      produce the assets the third consumes:
    </p>
    <div class="field-list">
      <div class="tier-row header">
        <span>Tier</span>
        <span>Job</span>
        <span>Produces</span>
        <span>Blocks until ready</span>
      </div>
      <div class="tier-row">
        <span>1</span>
        <span><strong>CalibrateStereo</strong></span>
        <span>A <code>CameraCalibration</code> asset from a checkerboard take, linked to the take and discoverable by the actor's other takes.</span>
        <span>—</span>
      </div>
      <div class="tier-row">
        <span>2</span>
        <span><strong>Identity Create</strong></span>
        <span>A rigged, prepared <code>MetaHumanIdentity</code> (<code>MHID_&lt;Actor&gt;</code>), built from an identity take using the calibration above.</span>
        <span>Needs the calibration (for depth). Runs in an interactive editor (cloud AutoRig login).</span>
      </div>
      <div class="tier-row">
        <span>3</span>
        <span><strong>Stereo performance</strong></span>
        <span>A <code>Performance</code> asset + face <code>AnimSequence</code> — a depth-constrained solve.</span>
        <span>Needs <strong>both</strong> the calibration and the prepared identity for its actor.</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="callout callout-note">
      <strong>The iron rule:</strong> a stereo take never processes without both parent assets for its
      actor. Depth is generated from the calibration before the solve, so a bad calibration would
      poison every take built on it — which is why the <a href="/marketplace/mha-manager/docs/global-settings">calibration quality gates</a>
      sit upstream. Mono and Audio jobs depend on neither and never wait.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Queue in any order</h2>
    <p class="section-desc">
      Execution order ignores queue row order. The batch always runs
      <strong>CalibrateStereo first, Identity Create second, everything else third</strong>, so a
      take's prerequisites exist by the time it preflights. The queue is strictly serial (Epic's
      single-performance lock), and takes are classified automatically by name — <code>grid</code> /
      <code>calib</code> tokens become CalibrateStereo, <code>identity</code> / <code>dna</code> become
      Identity Create. That means you can drop a whole actor folder into the queue at once and let the
      tiers sort themselves out.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Worked example — one actor folder</h2>
    <p class="section-desc">
      An actor <strong>Aaron</strong> was shot with a checkerboard take, an identity take, and several
      performance takes, all under <code>Imports/Aaron/</code>. Queue everything, in any order, and
      press Start:
    </p>
    <ol class="numbered-list">
      <li><code>Aaron_Calib_Grid</code> runs first as a <strong>CalibrateStereo</strong> job — producing a Camera Calibration asset next to the take.</li>
      <li><code>Aaron_Identity_DNA</code> runs second as <strong>Identity Create</strong>: depth is generated with that calibration, the neutral frame is tracked and conformed, cloud AutoRig runs, and Prepare for Performance produces <code>MHID_Aaron</code>.</li>
      <li><code>Aaron_Take01…N</code> run last as <strong>Stereo</strong> jobs: preflight links the calibration and <code>MHID_Aaron</code>, the diagnostics gate passes, and each take produces a Performance asset and AnimSequence.</li>
      <li>Any take you happened to queue <em>before</em> its prerequisites simply waits as a blocked status and resumes automatically once the parent completes — the tiers force grid -&gt; identity -&gt; performances regardless of the order you added them.</li>
    </ol>
    <figure class="doc-figure">
      <div class="doc-img-placeholder">
        <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="28" height="28" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" class="placeholder-icon"><rect x="3" y="3" width="18" height="18" rx="2"/><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="4"/><line x1="20" y1="4" x2="20" y2="4.01"/></svg>
        <span class="placeholder-name">stereo-actor-queue.webp</span>
        <span class="placeholder-hint">Queue with a full actor folder loaded — a Calibration job, an Identity Create job, and several Stereo take rows, some showing a Missing* blocked status waiting on their parents</span>
      </div>
      <figcaption>A full actor folder queued at once — calibration and identity run first, performance takes follow</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Discovery and blocked states</h2>
    <p class="section-desc">
      Discovery is scoped to the actor's folders, which is why keeping a shoot's takes under one
      actor folder matters:
    </p>
    <ul class="bullet-list">
      <li><strong>Calibration lookup:</strong> already linked on the Capture Data -&gt; capture parent folder -&gt; take folder -&gt; sibling take folders -&gt; one level higher, never past <code>/Game</code>.</li>
      <li><strong>Identity lookup:</strong> capture parent folder -&gt; actor folder, preferring <code>MHID_&lt;Actor&gt;</code>.</li>
      <li><strong>Rejected calibrations</strong> (set aside for poor quality) are invisible to every lookup until you accept or re-solve them.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="section-desc">A blocked take shows one of three statuses, and each clears differently:</p>
    <div class="field-list">
      <div class="blk-row header">
        <span>Status</span>
        <span>Meaning &amp; how it clears</span>
      </div>
      <div class="blk-row">
        <span><strong>MissingCalibration</strong></span>
        <span>No calibration found for the actor yet. Requeues automatically the moment one appears (a Calibration job finishes, or you link the asset).</span>
      </div>
      <div class="blk-row">
        <span><strong>MissingIdentity</strong></span>
        <span>No prepared identity found for the actor yet. Requeues automatically once one exists.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="blk-row">
        <span><strong>PoorCalibrationQuality</strong></span>
        <span>A calibration solved but exceeded a quality gate and was set aside. Waits for an explicit decision — <strong>Accept Anyway</strong>, or a better re-solve. See <a href="/marketplace/mha-manager/docs/global-settings">Calibration settings</a>.</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="doc-footer">
    <a href="/marketplace/mha-manager/docs/job-types" class="prev-link">
